	(text "No, the schematic recipe I provided above is **Fixed Gain (10x)**. It uses a standard  resistor, so the zoom level is locked.\n\nSince you are removing the MAX9939 (which *was* a variable gain amplifier), you likely want to keep that functionality—especially since Nanopores vary wildly in current. A 1 nA pore needs different gain than a 100 pA pore.\n\nHere is how to build a **\"Safe\" Variable Gain Amplifier (PGA)** that creates that necessary 2.5V offset but allows you to switch zoom levels (e.g., 10x, 50x, 100x) using your ESP32.\n\n### The Solution: The \"Mux-Switched\" Feedback Loop\n\nInstead of a single  resistor, you use an **Analog Multiplexer (Switch)** to select one of several resistors in the feedback loop.\n\n### Component List\n\n1. **Op-Amp:** **OPA192** (Same as before).\n2. **Analog Switch:** **TMUX1104** (or ADG704).\n* *Why:* Extremely low capacitance and leakage. It acts like a digital rotary switch.\n\n\n3. **Resistor Bank (The \"Zoom\" Levels):**\n* **R_FB1:**  (Gain 10x) - *For big pores / baseline.*\n* **R_FB2:**  (Gain 50x) - *For typical sensing.*\n* **R_FB3:**  (Gain 100x) - *For tiny pores / DNA events.*\n* **R_FB4:**  (Gain 200x) - *Max Zoom.*\n\n\n\n### The Circuit Connections\n\nThis modifies the \"Level Shifter\" circuit I gave you earlier:\n\n1. **Input Path (Unchanged):**\n* TIA Output   Resistor  OPA192 Pin 2 (Inverting Input).\n\n\n2. **Bias Path (Unchanged):**\n* 2.5V Ref  Divider ()  OPA192 Pin 3 (Non-Inverting).\n* *Target:* Keep Pin 3 at **0.227V**.\n\n\n3. **The Feedback Path (The Change):**\n* Connect the **Common Pin (D)** of the TMUX1104 to the **Op-Amp Output (Pin 6)**.\n* Connect **Source Pin S1** to **R_FB1** ().\n* Connect **Source Pin S2** to **R_FB2** ().\n* Connect **Source Pin S3** to **R_FB3** ().\n* Connect **Source Pin S4** to **R_FB4** ().\n* *Tie the other ends of all four resistors together* and connect them to **OPA192 Pin 2 (Inverting Input)**.\n\n\n\n### Why this is better than the MAX9939\n\n* **Bipolar Safety:** Because we are summing into the *Inverting Node* (Pin 2) which is held at 0.227V, the switch never sees negative voltages. It is safe from the \"clipping\" issue that kills the MAX9939.\n* **Bandwidth:** The OPA192 has plenty of speed (10 MHz). Even at 100x gain, you have 100 kHz bandwidth.\n* **Control:** Your ESP32 simply toggles 2 GPIO pins (connected to the TMUX1104 address lines) to change the gain instantly from \"Overview\" to \"Deep Zoom.\""
		(exclude_from_sim no)
