	(text "Using an **Optically Isolated Current Source (LED + Photodiode)** is the \"Ferrari\" of dummy loads for nanopore amplifiers.\n\nWhile the resistor method (like in the EarlGrey PDF) is great for checking baseline noise, the LED/Photodiode method is superior for checking **bandwidth** and **step response** (simulating DNA events) because it solves the \"Probe Capacitance\" problem.\n\nHere is why the optical method is the secret weapon for verifying your 100 kHz bandwidth, and how to build one.\n\n### 1. The Physics: Why Resistors Lie\n\nWhen you test your amplifier with a function generator and a resistor:\n\n* **The Problem:** You have to connect a BNC cable from your Function Generator to the resistor. That cable adds **~50–100 pF** of capacitance to your sensitive input node.\n* **The Result:** You are testing the *cable's* bandwidth, not your amplifier's bandwidth. The heavy capacitance of the test gear hides the true speed of your PTFE/Rogers board.\n\n### 2. The Solution: The Optical Current Injector\n\nA photodiode acts as a **True Current Source** (Infinite Impedance), just like a real nanopore.\n\n* **How it works:**\n1. **Transmitter (Noisy Side):** You drive an LED with a function generator. You can pulse it to simulate a 100 kHz DNA event.\n2. **Receiver (Quiet Side):** A dark, shielded photodiode sits directly on your TIA input. It \"sees\" the light and generates a proportional current (e.g., 100 pA).\n\n\n* **The Magic:** There is **zero electrical connection** between the function generator and your amplifier. No ground loops. No cable capacitance. You are injecting pure signal directly into the amp.\n\n### 3. How to Build the \"Simulator Puck\"\n\nYou should build a dedicated \"Test Puck\" that looks exactly like your fluidic puck but contains electronics instead of water.\n\n**Components:**\n\n1. **LED:** A standard fast Red LED (e.g., Kingbright).\n2. **Photodiode:** You need a **PIN Photodiode** with extremely low capacitance ().\n* *Recommendation:* **Hamamatsu S1223** (or similar small-area silicon PIN).\n* *Warning:* Do not use a large solar cell; its capacitance will be huge (nF range) and ruin the test.\n\n\n\n**The Circuit:**\n\n* **Input (LED):** Connect the LED to a BNC connector on the *outside* of the shield.\n* **Output (PD):** Solder the Photodiode directly between the **Sense Input** and **Ground** (or Bias Voltage) on a spare copy of your Rogers Carrier Board.\n* **Optical Coupling:** Drill a hole in the PTFE board so the LED (Top) shines down on the Photodiode (Bottom). Seal it light-tight with black epoxy.\n\n### 4. Calibration Strategy\n\nThe only downside to optical testing is that you don't know exactly what \"5V\" on the LED equals in picoamps.\n\n**The \"Hybrid\" Dummy Load (Best Practice):**\nDesign your \"Simulator Puck\" with **both**:\n\n1. **A 1 G$\\Omega$ Resistor:** Connected to a stable DC voltage source.\n* *Use:* Sets the \"Baseline Current\" (e.g., 10 nA) to simulate the open pore.\n\n\n2. **The LED/Photodiode:** In parallel.\n* *Use:* Injects small \"AC Pulses\" (e.g., 100 pA dips) on top of that baseline.\n\n\n\n**Test Procedure:**\n\n1. Turn on the DC source. Your Amp reads 10 nA. (Verifies DC gain).\n2. Flash the LED with a 10 µs pulse. Your Amp should see a sharp dip. (Verifies 100 kHz bandwidth).\n\n### Summary\n\n* **Resistor Load:** Good for noise checks. Bad for speed checks (due to cable capacitance).\n* **LED/Photodiode Load:** The only accurate way to verify 100 kHz bandwidth without a wet lab.\n\n**Recommendation:** Since you are building a custom PCB stack anyway, add a footprint for a small photodiode on your \"Carrier Board\" design. You can populate one board as a \"Simulator\" to tune your ESP32/ADC code before you ever mess with salt water."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
